          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I feel love for you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Goneril directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state and amplifies it through explicit comparisons. This is a clear avowal, regardless of whether it is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows a present feeling but does not itself promise future conduct, assume an obligation, or renew a relational undertaking. Lear's subsequent grant of territory is a response to the declaration, not an undertaking made by Goneril through these words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The declaration is part of an elaborate, solicited answer to Lear's question, rather than a reflex-like affective outburst. Its heightened rhetoric does not by itself establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"No residual core force beyond the avowal is needed. In context the longer statement also competes to answer who loves Lear most, but that comparative presentation remains part of its truth-presenting emotional claim.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]} },\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most while announcing that his largest bounty will follow where love and merit warrant it. Goneril, speaking first, gives the focal declaration as the opening of an elaborate claim that she loves him beyond ordinary measure.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal words contain no identifiable undertaking by Goneril. Lear treats her answer favorably and grants her and Albany's line a portion of the kingdom, but this is uptake of her answer in the staged distribution, not acceptance of a promise contained in “I love you.” The supplied scene gives reasons both to register the declaration as evidence of affection and to question its reliability: it is made under an announced material incentive, and Cordelia later challenges the sisters' claims. Sincerity is therefore not settled.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"This is the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare's King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied passage, not on remembered later events.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied passage does not establish whether Goneril's declaration is sincere or accurately measures her actual affection. Later conduct or revelations cannot be used here because they are not included in the evidence.\"}"
